@@@@ open-ieee754
situation: open
topic: IEEE 754 floating point
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"must":["0\\.1"],"askMustNot":["False","not equal","0\\.30000000000000004"]}
noverify: an opening; it asks what 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 gives and states no result
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Every number with a fractional part in C, Python, JavaScript and SQLite's REAL is stored in the IEEE 754 format, so its rules decide what your arithmetic returns. They are why money is kept in integer minor units rather than doubles, why a loss turns into NaN during training, and why summing the same numbers in a different order can change the last digit. Knowing the format lets you predict the error of a calculation instead of finding it in production, and it sits under the numerical work in ML and in pricing code.

<ask type="predict">In Python, what does `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` evaluate to? Say why you expect that.</ask>

@@@@ open-prior-pipeline-hazards
situation: open-prior
topic: Pipelining and hazards
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Pipelining and hazards","daysAgo":3,"finished":false,"plan":["The five stages","Data hazards and forwarding","Control hazards and branch prediction"],"right":3,"partly":0,"wrong":1,"missed":["Why can forwarding not remove the stall after a load that the very next instruction uses?"],"open":"A branch is resolved in the EX stage. How many instructions behind it have already been fetched?"}]}
expect: {"askMustNot":["\\bMEM\\b","one (cycle|stall|bubble)"],"must":["load"]}
noverify: an opening; the question's answer (value ready at the end of MEM, needed at the start of EX) is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Pipelining is how a processor overlaps the execution of several instructions, and hazards are the places where that overlap gives a wrong result unless the hardware steps in. Compilers order instructions around them, and the cost of a load followed by its use, or of a mispredicted branch, shows in the profile of any tight loop. You have the five stages and forwarding from last time; the load case was the one gap, and control hazards are still to come.

<ask type="short">In a five-stage pipeline, `lw x5, 0(x1)` is followed directly by `add x6, x5, x2`. At the end of which stage does the loaded value first exist, and at the start of which stage does the add need it?</ask>

